#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Rational-y Slice Lab for k=375 (Python 3.10+).

Equation:
    y^2 = 1 + (36*n^3 + 54*n^2 + 27*n - 58) / [x*(6*n+3+x)^2]
n,x are integers; y is allowed to be rational.

RUN WITH THE SAME MATPLOTLIB SETUP AS THE EARLIER EXPERIMENT
    python3 rational_y_lab.py
    python3 rational_y_lab.py --engine lattice --target cubes --mode tiles
    python3 rational_y_lab.py --engine lattice --target primitive --mode tiles
    python3 rational_y_lab.py --preset control
    python3 rational_y_lab.py --preset control --search-only
    python3 rational_y_lab.py --search-only --csv rational-points.csv

Matplotlib and NumPy are the only third-party dependencies for plots:
    python3 -m pip install matplotlib numpy
Search-only mode needs only Python's standard library.

LARGE-n VISUALIZATION: 10^15 <= n <= 10^16
    python3 rational_y_lab.py --preset large
    python3 rational_y_lab.py --preset large --slices 24 --mode residual
    python3 rational_y_lab.py --preset local-large --n 5000000000000000
    python3 rational_y_lab.py --preset large --engine scan --target rational --mode residual

The large preset selects 12 distinct admissible n values across this inclusive
interval using exact integer interpolation, followed by nearest n=2 or5 mod9.
It does NOT sweep all n: the interval contains 9,000,000,000,000,001 integers.
Each chosen n gets its own integer x window around
    x_center(n) = floor((6*n+3)/10), x_center +/- 5,000,000.
The default target is primitive, engine lattice, mode tiles, and tile width
1,000,000. Every integer x in each selected window is covered by the search.
CLI output and the companion -windows.csv name the exact searched windows.
No result is a claim about unselected n or about x outside these windows.

--n-stop makes --slices an integer sample count across [n,n-stop]. Without
--n-stop, n slices remain consecutive. Use --preset local-large for a local
consecutive window near a chosen large n. In sampled overviews, Previous/Next
sample changes the displayed 2D slices while retaining the full 3D stack.
In local windows, Previous/Next n shifts the window as before.

--x-center-ratio accepts exact fractions such as 1/10 or -3/2; the integer
centre is recomputed for each n. --x-half-width controls local search width.
Explicit --x-min and --x-max instead choose one fixed x interval for all n.
In large presets, --engine scan uses a smaller default half-width of5,000
to keep exact all-rational searches within the direct scan pair budget.

Offset axes subtract each slice's exact x and t origins BEFORE float conversion.
Overview depth is the sample index; exact n remains available in Terminal and
on point/tile selection. Normalized real curves almost coincide at this scale:
    y^2 = 1 + (1-375/S^3) / [6*u*(1+u)^2], u=x/S, S=6*n+3.
The correction 375/S^3 is about1.74e-45 down to1.74e-48 across this interval.
This visual coalescence does not weaken any exact arithmetic test.


TWO SEARCH ENGINES, THREE TARGETS
    scan + rational: every rational y at the integer n,x pairs in the window.
    scan + cubes/primitive: exact reference scan for integer cube lifts.
    lattice + cubes/primitive: certified tangent strips and exact 2D Gauss
        reduction in each fixed-n slice, followed by exact verification.

The lattice engine is a slice-wise ADAPTATION of the geometric idea in
Elkies / Elsenhans-Jahnel / Huisman. It uses genuine basis reduction and
enumeration of thin tangent strips. It is not the original optimized 3D
LLL + Fincke-Pohst program, nor a reproduction of a global 10^15 search.
Its finite n,x windows do not constitute a bound on all cubic triples.

EXACT RATIONAL TEST
    S=6n+3, T=S+x, N=(S^3-375)/6.
    H=x^2*T^2+x*N.
For x*T != 0:
    rational y <=> H>=0 is an integer square r^2;
    y=+/-r/abs(x*T), reduced exactly with Fraction.
There is no arbitrary denominator cutoff. We do NOT round a floating
square root or treat a near-square as an exact solution.

For the cubic goal additionally require t=y*T to be an integer.
At an exact rational point this is equivalent to x|N (or denominator(y)|T).
Then M=T^2+N/x must be a nonnegative INTEGER square t^2.

    a=-x-t, b=-x+t, c=S+2x
    a^3+b^3+c^3 = 375 - 6*[x*(t^2-T^2)-N].

Only zero residual is a solution. At a true integer-cube solution, a common
divisor g has g^3|375, so g is 1 or5. Primitive means gcd(a,b,c)=1.
Rational-only hits can give rational a,b,c and are not integer cube solutions.

MODULAR CONDITIONS
Integer-cube targets use necessary conditions:
    n mod9 in {2,5}; x mod3=1; t mod3=0.
More precisely t mod9=0 for n mod9=2, and t mod9 in{3,6} for n mod9=5.
There is NO restriction that n must be even.
Projected tables modulo8,5,7,13,19 are generated from
    x*t^2 = x*(S+x)^2+N (mod m).
They do not divide by x modulo m, so noninvertible residues are safe.

Allowed x mod8, indexed by n mod8:
    0:{2}, 1:{3}, 2:{1,3,4,5,7}, 3:{5},
    4:{6}, 5:{7}, 6:{0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7}, 7:{1}.
Modulo5, when n!=2 mod5, x=+/- (n+3) mod5 is necessary.
When n=2 mod5, all x residues survive this projected mod5 test.
If also 5|x, primitivity requires v5(x)=v5(N); x divisible by5 cannot
simply be banned. The final gcd check is always applied to exact hits.

These integer-lift filters are deliberately NOT applied to all-rational
mode. That mode only uses safe square-residue tests on H, followed by isqrt.

WHY THE LATTICE STRIPS ARE CERTIFIED
For a fixed n, t=f(x)=sqrt(g(x)), g(x)=(S+x)^2+N/x.
On I=[a,b] away from0, use interval bounds on g,g',g'' to bound |f''|.
A rational enclosure of f at the midpoint gives a rational tangent
approximation and a rigorous error width w. The full graph over I lies in
    |t-[t0+alpha*(x-x0)]| <= w.
Reduce the affine lattice (x,t)=(1,0)+Z*(3,0)+Z*(0,3), using the exact norm
    dx^2/h^2 + (dt-alpha*dx)^2/w^2.
Map strip corners through the reduced basis inverse, enumerate the integer
coefficient box, and verify candidates exactly. Unsafe or overly broad
tiles are subdivided; small pieces use an exact per-x fallback. This also
retains isolated real points. Work limits abort explicitly; no region is
silently skipped and then reported complete.

READING THE PLOTS
Points view: rational trial y-values (faint) and exact rational y hits.
Residual view: zero is the exact-hit target. The residual units differ:
    scan/rational uses E=r^2-H; integer-cube modes use R=x*(t^2-T^2)-N.
    E=x*R, so their numerical heights must not be compared as one scale.
Tiles view (lattice): certified parallelograms for the nonnegative |t| branch, with
normalized or exact-offset drawing coordinates and integer candidates.
Every displayed +/- pair is stored once with nonnegative |y|.

Previous/Next selects samples in an overview or shifts a local n window. View cycles the available views.
Clicking a point prints its full exact details in Terminal.
Drag the 3D axes to rotate; use Matplotlib's toolbar to zoom/save.
Large-coordinate drawing is approximate; --axis offset subtracts per-slice origins
before float conversion when using sampled or ratio-centred windows. Exact decisions and CSV values retain full integers.
Drawing limits only downsample nonexact points; every exact hit is retained.

EXAMPLES / CONTROLS
    n=2,x=16: y=+/-63/62, but t=+/-63/2: rational cubes only.
    n=2,x=-36: y=+/-62/63: rational cubes only.
    n=2,x=-20 and -5: y=0: integer cubes, gcd5, nonprimitive.

The control preset searches a window around a known NONPRIMITIVE large
point obtained by scaling Booker-Sutherland's cubic-3 solution by5.
It is a validation control, not a new discovery. The point is found by
the search; it is not injected into the output or accepted without checks.

COVERAGE
Every integer cubic375 triple can be permuted to choose same-parity a,b.
Then n=(a+b+c-3)/6 and x=-(a+b)/2 are integers, and
    y=(b-a)/(a+b+2c)
is rational. Cubes mod9 force a,b,c=-1 mod3, so x and S+x cannot be0.
Thus rational y with integral t covers the integer cubic problem up to
permutation, but any finite window still searches only that window.
Both positive and negative n must be considered in a broader investigation.

REFERENCES (PRIMARY SOURCES)
S. G. Huisman, Newer sums of three cubes (2016), section2:
    https://arxiv.org/pdf/1604.07746
A.-S. Elsenhans and J. Jahnel, New sums of three cubes (2009):
    https://wwwuser.gwdguser.de/~jjahnel/Arbeiten/elk_ants6c.pdf
N. D. Elkies, Rational points near curves ... via lattice reduction (2000):
    https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0005139
A. R. Booker and A. V. Sutherland, On a question of Mordell (2021),
source of the cubic3 solution used only for the nonprimitive control:
    https://arxiv.org/pdf/2007.01209
"""

from __future__ import annotations


# Rational Core

"""Exact arithmetic for rational-y slices of the k=375 equation.

This module has no third-party dependencies. There are deliberately two
different residuals: kind='r' uses E=r^2-H; kind='t' uses the original
R=x(t^2-(S+x)^2)-N. E=x*R. Do not compare their numerical heights.

Integer-cube filters must not be used for all rational points. In particular
n need not be even, and rational y alone does not make the cube triple integral.
"""

import csv
from dataclasses import dataclass
from fractions import Fraction
from math import gcd, isqrt
from pathlib import Path


def coeffs(n: int) -> tuple[int, int]:
    return 6 * n + 3, 36 * n**3 + 54 * n**2 + 27 * n - 58


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Record:
    n: int
    x: int
    y: Fraction
    aux: int
    residual: int
    kind: str

    @property
    def exact(self) -> bool:
        return self.residual == 0

    @property
    def t(self) -> Fraction:
        return self.y * (6 * self.n + 3 + self.x)

    @property
    def cube_triple(self) -> tuple[Fraction, Fraction, Fraction]:
        s = 6 * self.n + 3
        return Fraction(-self.x) - self.t, Fraction(-self.x) + self.t, Fraction(s + 2 * self.x)

    @property
    def integral_lift(self) -> bool:
        return self.exact and self.t.denominator == 1

    @property
    def cube_gcd(self) -> int | None:
        if not self.integral_lift:
            return None
        return gcd(*(v.numerator for v in self.cube_triple))

    @property
    def primitive(self) -> bool:
        return self.integral_lift and self.cube_gcd == 1

    @property
    def original_residual(self) -> Fraction:
        return Fraction(self.residual, self.x) if self.kind == "r" else Fraction(self.residual)

    @property
    def status(self) -> str:
        if not self.exact:
            return "candidate"
        if not self.integral_lift:
            return "rational_only"
        return "primitive_cubes" if self.primitive else "nonprimitive_cubes"

    def detail(self) -> str:
        labels = {
            "candidate": "NEARBY CANDIDATE; not a solution",
            "rational_only": "EXACT RATIONAL y; cube triple is not integral",
            "nonprimitive_cubes": "EXACT INTEGER-CUBE LIFT; nonprimitive",
            "primitive_cubes": "EXACT PRIMITIVE INTEGER-CUBE SOLUTION",
        }
        y_label = "0" if self.y == 0 else f"±{self.y}"
        residual_label = "E = r² − H" if self.kind == "r" else "R = x(t² − T²) − N"
        lines = [f"{labels[self.status]}: n={self.n}, x={self.x}, y={y_label}",
                 f"{residual_label} = {self.residual}; t=yT={self.t} for the positive-y branch"]
        if self.kind == "t":
            lines.append(f"Lattice view uses |t|={self.aux}; changing the sign exchanges the first two cubes.")
        if self.exact:
            triple = ", ".join(str(v) for v in self.cube_triple)
            suffix = f"; gcd={self.cube_gcd}" if self.integral_lift else "; rational cubes only"
            lines.append(f"(a,b,c)=({triple}); a³+b³+c³=375{suffix}")
        else:
            lines.append(f"Original equation residual R={self.original_residual}; exact acceptance requires zero.")
        return "\n".join(lines)


def make_r_record(n: int, x: int, r: int) -> Record:
    """Use r>=0 on the denominator grid y=r/|xT|, retaining exact integers."""
    s, numerator = coeffs(n)
    tbase = s + x
    if x == 0 or tbase == 0:
        raise ValueError("The original equation excludes x=0 and x=-(6n+3).")
    if r < 0:
        raise ValueError("r must be nonnegative; each record represents both signs of y.")
    h = x * x * tbase * tbase + x * numerator
    return Record(n, x, Fraction(r, abs(x * tbase)), r, r * r - h, "r")


def make_t_record(n: int, x: int, t: int) -> Record:
    """Use |t|>=0 with y=|t/T|; the two signs swap the first two cubes."""
    s, numerator = coeffs(n)
    tbase = s + x
    if x == 0 or tbase == 0:
        raise ValueError("The original equation excludes x=0 and x=-(6n+3).")
    if t < 0:
        raise ValueError("t must be nonnegative.")
    return Record(n, x, abs(Fraction(t, tbase)), t, x * (t * t - tbase * tbase) - numerator, "t")


def square_residues(modulus: int) -> frozenset[int]:
    return frozenset(t * t % modulus for t in range(modulus))


def projected_lift_table(modulus: int) -> tuple[int, ...]:
    """Bitset of x residues admitting some t modulo modulus, for each n.

    This uses multiplication, never modular division, so nonunits are safe.
    A bit x is set exactly when x*t² == x*(S+x)²+N has a local solution.
    """
    squares = square_residues(modulus)
    table = []
    for n in range(modulus):
        s, numerator = coeffs(n)
        row = 0
        for x in range(modulus):
            required = (x * (s + x)**2 + numerator) % modulus
            if any(x * q % modulus == required for q in squares):
                row |= 1 << x
        table.append(row)
    return tuple(table)


LIFT_MODULI = (8, 5, 7, 13, 19)
LIFT_TABLES = {m: projected_lift_table(m) for m in LIFT_MODULI}
RATIONAL_MODULI = (16, 27, 25, 7, 11, 13)
RATIONAL_SQUARES = {m: square_residues(m) for m in RATIONAL_MODULI}


def valuation5(value: int) -> int:
    """5-adic valuation of a nonzero integer."""
    value = abs(value)
    if value == 0:
        raise ValueError("valuation5 requires a nonzero integer")
    result = 0
    while value % 5 == 0:
        value //= 5
        result += 1
    return result


def cheap_lift_allowed(n: int, x: int, primitive: bool = False) -> bool:
    """Necessary local conditions only; this deliberately does NOT test x|N.

    Modulo 27 implies n=2 or5 mod9, x=1 mod3 and t=0 mod3.
    Additional tables project the exact congruence to (n,x).
    For a primitive solution with 5|S and 5|x, t must be a 5-adic unit,
    forcing v5(x)=v5(N). No n-even restriction is valid here.
    """
    s, numerator = coeffs(n)
    if x == 0 or s + x == 0:
        return False
    if n % 9 not in (2, 5) or x % 3 != 1:
        return False
    for modulus, table in LIFT_TABLES.items():
        if not (table[n % modulus] >> (x % modulus)) & 1:
            return False
    if primitive and s % 5 == 0 and x % 5 == 0:
        if valuation5(x) != valuation5(numerator):
            return False
    return True


def rational_hit(n: int, x: int) -> Record | None:
    """All rational y: H must be an exact nonnegative integer square."""
    s, numerator = coeffs(n)
    if x == 0 or s + x == 0:
        return None
    h = x * x * (s + x)**2 + x * numerator
    if h < 0:
        return None
    r = isqrt(h)
    return make_r_record(n, x, r) if r * r == h else None


def admissible_hit(n: int, x: int, primitive: bool = False) -> Record | None:
    """Exact integral-cube lift, optionally primitive; local tests are necessary."""
    if not cheap_lift_allowed(n, x, primitive):
        return None
    s, numerator = coeffs(n)
    if numerator % x:
        return None
    m = (s + x)**2 + numerator // x
    if m < 0:
        return None
    t = isqrt(m)
    if t * t != m:
        return None
    record = make_t_record(n, x, t)
    if primitive and not record.primitive:
        return None
    return record


def scan_window(n_start: int, slices: int, x_min: int, x_max: int,
                target: str = "rational", max_pairs: int = 300000) -> dict:
    """Exhaustive finite (n,x) rectangle, with safely sieved visual candidates.

    Rational mode uses adjacent integer r to sqrt(H); exact points cover ALL
    rational y in the rectangle. Cubes/primitive use x|N, then adjacent
    multiples of 3 to sqrt(M), since every exact integral lift has 3|t.
    Nonzero candidates are included only to visualize the zero-residual test.
    """
    if target not in {"rational", "cubes", "primitive"}:
        raise ValueError("target must be rational, cubes, or primitive")
    if slices < 1 or x_min > x_max:
        raise ValueError("Require positive slices and x_min <= x_max")
    count = slices * (x_max - x_min + 1)
    if count > max_pairs:
        raise ValueError(f"This finite sample has {count:,} pairs; limit is {max_pairs:,}.")
    stats = dict(tested_pairs=0, poles=0, modular_rejected=0, modular_pass=0,
                 divisibility_rejected=0, divisor_pass=0, nonreal=0,
                 nonprimitive_rejected=0, exact_hits=0, rational_only_hits=0,
                 integral_hits=0, primitive_hits=0)
    records = []
    for n in range(n_start, n_start + slices):
        s, numerator = coeffs(n)
        for x in range(x_min, x_max + 1):
            stats["tested_pairs"] += 1
            tbase = s + x
            if x == 0 or tbase == 0:
                stats["poles"] += 1
                continue
            if target == "rational":
                h = x * x * tbase * tbase + x * numerator
                if h < 0:
                    stats["nonreal"] += 1
                    continue
                if any(h % modulus not in squares for modulus, squares in RATIONAL_SQUARES.items()):
                    stats["modular_rejected"] += 1
                    continue
                stats["modular_pass"] += 1
                lower = isqrt(h)
                roots = (lower,) if lower * lower == h else (lower, lower + 1)
                selected = [make_r_record(n, x, r) for r in roots]
            else:
                if not cheap_lift_allowed(n, x, target == "primitive"):
                    stats["modular_rejected"] += 1
                    continue
                stats["modular_pass"] += 1
                if numerator % x:
                    stats["divisibility_rejected"] += 1
                    continue
                stats["divisor_pass"] += 1
                m = tbase * tbase + numerator // x
                if m < 0:
                    stats["nonreal"] += 1
                    continue
                lower = 3 * (isqrt(m) // 3)
                roots = (lower,) if lower * lower == m else (lower, lower + 3)
                selected = [make_t_record(n, x, t) for t in roots]
            for record in selected:
                if target == "primitive" and record.exact and not record.primitive:
                    stats["nonprimitive_rejected"] += 1
                    continue
                records.append(record)
                if record.exact:
                    stats["exact_hits"] += 1
                    stats["integral_hits"] += int(record.integral_lift)
                    stats["primitive_hits"] += int(record.primitive)
                    stats["rational_only_hits"] += int(not record.integral_lift)
    stats["records"] = len(records)
    stats["target"] = target
    return {"records": records, "stats": stats, "tiles": []}


def export_csv(path: str | Path, records) -> None:
    """Write exact rational strings; each row represents both signs of y."""
    path = Path(path)
    path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    with path.open("w", encoding="utf-8", newline="") as handle:
        writer = csv.writer(handle)
        writer.writerow(["n", "x", "y_numerator", "y_denominator", "y_abs_exact",
                         "t_numerator", "t_denominator", "aux", "residual_kind",
                         "residual", "original_residual", "exact", "integral_lift",
                         "primitive", "status", "a", "b", "c", "cube_sum", "gcd"])
        for record in records:
            triple = record.cube_triple if record.exact else ("", "", "")
            total = sum(v**3 for v in triple) if record.exact else ""
            writer.writerow([record.n, record.x, record.y.numerator, record.y.denominator,
                             str(record.y), record.t.numerator, record.t.denominator,
                             record.aux, "r_squared_minus_H" if record.kind == "r" else "original_R",
                             record.residual, str(record.original_residual), record.exact,
                             record.integral_lift, record.primitive, record.status,
                             *map(str, triple), str(total), record.cube_gcd if record.integral_lift else ""])


# Rational Lattice

"""Certified two-dimensional tangent tiles for the cubic-375 slices.

The output points are integer candidates (x,t), t>=0, in the necessary
affine sublattice x=1 mod 3, t=0 mod 3. They are NOT all exact solutions.
Every exact integral-cube lift in the requested finite interval is retained.
All geometry and acceptance decisions use integers and Fraction.

This is a slice-wise adaptation of the tangent-tile/lattice-reduction idea
of Elkies / Elsenhans--Jahnel / Huisman, not their original 3D implementation.
"""

from fractions import Fraction
from math import gcd, isqrt


class WorkLimitError(RuntimeError):
    """The finite search was stopped; it must not be reported as complete."""

    def __init__(self, message, stats=None):
        super().__init__(message)
        self.stats = dict(stats or {})
        self.stats["complete"] = False


def _floor(q):
    return q.numerator // q.denominator


def _ceil(q):
    return -((-q.numerator) // q.denominator)


def _nearest_ratio(num, den):
    """Nearest integer, ties toward +infinity; den must be positive."""
    return (2 * num + den) // (2 * den)


def _sqrt_interval(q, bits):
    """Certified rational enclosure of sqrt(q), including exact squares."""
    if q < 0:
        raise ValueError("Cannot enclose a negative square root")
    if q == 0:
        return Fraction(0), Fraction(0)
    pn, qn = isqrt(q.numerator), isqrt(q.denominator)
    if pn * pn == q.numerator and qn * qn == q.denominator:
        exact = Fraction(pn, qn)
        return exact, exact
    scale = 1 << bits
    k = isqrt((q.numerator * scale * scale) // q.denominator)
    return Fraction(k, scale), Fraction(k + 1, scale)


def _gauss_basis(alpha, halfwidth, stripwidth):
    """Reduce the affine sublattice's basis in an exact weighted metric.

    The metric is proportional to
        dx^2 / halfwidth^2 + (dt-alpha*dx)^2 / stripwidth^2.
    Clearing denominators gives integer dot products, avoiding repeated
    Fraction normalization during the reduction.
    """
    an, ad = alpha.numerator, alpha.denominator
    hn, hd = halfwidth.numerator, halfwidth.denominator
    wn, wd = stripwidth.numerator, stripwidth.denominator
    p = wn * wn * hd * hd * ad * ad
    q = hn * hn * wd * wd
    common = gcd(p, q)
    p //= common
    q //= common

    def dot(v, z):
        return p * v[0] * z[0] + q * (ad * v[1] - an * v[0]) * (ad * z[1] - an * z[0])

    b1, b2 = (3, 0), (0, 3)
    for iteration in range(1024):
        norm1, norm2 = dot(b1, b1), dot(b2, b2)
        if norm2 < norm1:
            b1, b2 = b2, b1
            norm1 = norm2
        mu = _nearest_ratio(dot(b1, b2), norm1)
        if mu == 0:
            return b1, b2, iteration + 1
        b2 = (b2[0] - mu * b1[0], b2[1] - mu * b1[1])
    raise WorkLimitError("Lattice reduction iteration limit reached; search incomplete.")


def _make_tile(n, s, numerator, a, b, sqrt_bits):
    """Return a certified tangent strip, or None if subdivision is needed."""
    if a >= b or a <= 0 <= b:
        return None
    aa, bb = Fraction(a), Fraction(b)
    center = (aa + bb) / 2
    halfwidth = (bb - aa) / 2
    sa, sb = s + aa, s + bb
    min_square = Fraction(0) if sa <= 0 <= sb else min(sa * sa, sb * sb)
    g_lower = min_square + min(Fraction(numerator, a), Fraction(numerator, b))
    if g_lower <= 0:
        return None
    root_lower, _ = _sqrt_interval(g_lower, sqrt_bits)
    if root_lower <= 0:
        return None
    distance = min(abs(aa), abs(bb))
    amplitude = max(abs(sa), abs(sb))
    g1_bound = 2 * amplitude + Fraction(abs(numerator), 1) / distance**2
    g2_bound = 2 + 2 * Fraction(abs(numerator), 1) / distance**3
    curvature = g2_bound / (2 * root_lower) + g1_bound**2 / (4 * root_lower**3)
    g_center = (s + center)**2 + numerator / center
    root_lo, root_hi = _sqrt_interval(g_center, sqrt_bits)
    if root_lo <= 0:
        return None
    t_center = (root_lo + root_hi) / 2
    e0 = (root_hi - root_lo) / 2
    g1_center = 2 * (s + center) - numerator / center**2
    alpha = g1_center / (2 * t_center)
    e1 = abs(g1_center) * e0 / (2 * root_lo * t_center)
    width = e0 + halfwidth * e1 + curvature * halfwidth**2 / 2
    if width <= 0:
        return None
    b1, b2, iterations = _gauss_basis(alpha, halfwidth, width)
    determinant = b1[0] * b2[1] - b2[0] * b1[1]
    if abs(determinant) != 9:
        raise ArithmeticError("Reduction changed the lattice determinant")
    corners = []
    for x_corner in (aa, bb):
        line = t_center + alpha * (x_corner - center)
        for t_corner in (line - width, line + width):
            shifted_x = x_corner - 1
            k1 = (b2[1] * shifted_x - b2[0] * t_corner) / determinant
            k2 = (-b1[1] * shifted_x + b1[0] * t_corner) / determinant
            corners.append((k1, k2))
    k1_lo, k1_hi = _ceil(min(v[0] for v in corners)), _floor(max(v[0] for v in corners))
    k2_lo, k2_hi = _ceil(min(v[1] for v in corners)), _floor(max(v[1] for v in corners))
    box_nodes = max(0, k1_hi - k1_lo + 1) * max(0, k2_hi - k2_lo + 1)
    return {
        "n": n, "a": a, "b": b, "x0": center, "t0": t_center,
        "alpha": alpha, "w": width, "h": halfwidth,
        "basis": [b1, b2], "offset": (1, 0),
        "k1_min": k1_lo, "k1_max": k1_hi,
        "k2_min": k2_lo, "k2_max": k2_hi,
        "box_nodes": box_nodes, "reduction_steps": iterations,
        "curvature_bound": curvature, "kind": "certified_tangent",
    }


def lattice_slice(n, x_min, x_max, tile_width=64, max_nodes=200000, sqrt_bits=48):
    """Return certified finite-window integral-lift candidates for one n.

    Output:
        points: sorted unique (x,t) candidates, t>=0.
        tiles: certified tangent-strip dictionaries, all geometry exact.
        stats: work counts, exact-root count, and complete=True on success.
        fallback_points: candidates generated by exact per-x fallback.

    Tiles use x=1 (mod3), t=0 (mod3). n not congruent to2 (mod3) is
    rejected by a necessary condition for ANY integer cubic375 lift.
    No stronger modular or primitive filters are applied here.

    Fallback includes the neighboring multiples of3 around sqrt(g(x)).
    Those points are only visualization candidates. Every exact target
    root survives this choice. Work limits raise WorkLimitError rather
    than returning a misleading partial result.
    """
    if x_min > x_max:
        raise ValueError("Require x_min <= x_max")
    if tile_width < 1:
        raise ValueError("tile_width must be positive")
    if max_nodes < 1:
        raise ValueError("max_nodes must be positive")
    if not 8 <= sqrt_bits <= 256:
        raise ValueError("sqrt_bits must be between8 and256")
    s = 6 * n + 3
    numerator = 36 * n**3 + 54 * n**2 + 27 * n - 58
    stats = {
        "complete": True, "n_mod3_rejected": n % 3 != 2,
        "requested_x": x_max - x_min + 1, "nodes": 0,
        "lattice_nodes": 0, "fallback_x": 0, "fallback_blocks": 0,
        "tile_attempts": 0, "certified_tiles": 0, "subdivisions": 0,
        "gauss_steps": 0, "candidate_points": 0, "exact_roots": 0,
        "nonreal_fallback_x": 0, "pole_fallback_x": 0,
    }
    if n % 3 != 2:
        return {"points": [], "tiles": [], "stats": stats, "fallback_points": []}
    max_tiles = min(20000, max(1000, max_nodes))
    initial_tiles = (x_max - x_min + tile_width) // tile_width
    if initial_tiles > max_tiles:
        raise WorkLimitError(
            f"Requested interval needs {initial_tiles:,} initial tiles, above the "
            f"{max_tiles:,} limit. Increase tile_width or narrow the interval; search incomplete.",
            stats,
        )
    points, fallback_points, exact_roots = set(), set(), set()
    tiles = []

    def consume(count, kind):
        if stats["nodes"] + count > max_nodes:
            raise WorkLimitError(
                f"Work limit of {max_nodes:,} nodes reached; search incomplete. "
                "Narrow the range or increase max_nodes.", stats,
            )
        stats["nodes"] += count
        stats[kind] += count

    def record(x, t, fallback=False):
        if x == 0 or s + x == 0 or t < 0:
            return
        if x % 3 != 1 or t % 3:
            raise ArithmeticError("Candidate escaped the necessary sublattice")
        point = (x, t)
        points.add(point)
        if fallback:
            fallback_points.add(point)
        if x * (t * t - (s + x)**2) == numerator:
            exact_roots.add(point)

    def fallback(a, b):
        stats["fallback_blocks"] += 1
        first = a + ((1 - a) % 3)
        for x in range(first, b + 1, 3):
            consume(1, "fallback_x")
            if x == 0 or s + x == 0:
                stats["pole_fallback_x"] += 1
                continue
            g = Fraction(x * (s + x)**2 + numerator, x)
            if g < 0:
                stats["nonreal_fallback_x"] += 1
                continue
            lower = 3 * (isqrt(g.numerator // g.denominator) // 3)
            record(x, lower, True)
            if lower * lower * g.denominator != g.numerator:
                record(x, lower + 3, True)

    cursor = x_min
    while cursor <= x_max:
        stop = min(cursor + tile_width - 1, x_max)
        stack = [(cursor, stop)]
        cursor = stop + 1
        while stack:
            a, b = stack.pop()
            stats["tile_attempts"] += 1
            if stats["tile_attempts"] > max_tiles:
                raise WorkLimitError(
                    f"Tile limit of {max_tiles:,} reached; search incomplete. Narrow the range.",
                    stats,
                )
            count = b - a + 1
            if count <= 4:
                fallback(a, b)
                continue
            tile = _make_tile(n, s, numerator, a, b, sqrt_bits)
            tile_budget = max(32, min(4096, max_nodes - stats["nodes"]))
            if tile is None or tile["box_nodes"] > tile_budget:
                # Exact fallback is cheaper near poles or real-domain boundaries.
                # At larger scales subdividing keeps the geometric enumeration useful.
                if count <= 12:
                    fallback(a, b)
                else:
                    mid = (a + b) // 2
                    stack.extend(((mid + 1, b), (a, mid)))
                    stats["subdivisions"] += 1
                continue
            tiles.append(tile)
            stats["certified_tiles"] += 1
            stats["gauss_steps"] += tile["reduction_steps"]
            b1, b2 = tile["basis"]
            for k1 in range(tile["k1_min"], tile["k1_max"] + 1):
                for k2 in range(tile["k2_min"], tile["k2_max"] + 1):
                    consume(1, "lattice_nodes")
                    x = 1 + b1[0] * k1 + b2[0] * k2
                    t = b1[1] * k1 + b2[1] * k2
                    if not a <= x <= b or t < 0:
                        continue
                    line = tile["t0"] + tile["alpha"] * (x - tile["x0"])
                    if abs(t - line) <= tile["w"]:
                        record(x, t)
    stats["candidate_points"] = len(points)
    stats["exact_roots"] = len(exact_roots)
    return {
        "points": sorted(points), "tiles": tiles, "stats": stats,
        "fallback_points": sorted(fallback_points),
    }


def _lattice_self_check():
    """Targeted oracle checks; run this module directly to execute them."""
    def brute(n, lo, hi):
        s = 6 * n + 3
        numerator = 36 * n**3 + 54 * n**2 + 27 * n - 58
        answer = set()
        for x in range(lo, hi + 1):
            if x == 0 or s + x == 0:
                continue
            if numerator % x:
                continue
            value = (s + x)**2 + numerator // x
            if value < 0:
                continue
            t = isqrt(value)
            if t * t == value:
                answer.add((x, t))
        return answer

    for n in range(-10, 15):
        lo, hi = -220, 220
        result = lattice_slice(n, lo, hi, tile_width=43)
        s = 6 * n + 3
        numerator = 36 * n**3 + 54 * n**2 + 27 * n - 58
        found = {
            (x, t) for x, t in result["points"]
            if x * (t * t - (s + x)**2) == numerator
        }
        assert found == brute(n, lo, hi), (n, found, brute(n, lo, hi))
        for tile in result["tiles"]:
            b1, b2 = tile["basis"]
            assert abs(b1[0] * b2[1] - b2[0] * b1[1]) == 9
            # Integer samples must lie inside their certified strip whenever real.
            for x in range(tile["a"], tile["b"] + 1):
                if x == 0:
                    continue
                g = Fraction(x * (s + x)**2 + numerator, x)
                if g < 0:
                    continue
                l, u = _sqrt_interval(g, 96)
                line = tile["t0"] + tile["alpha"] * (x - tile["x0"])
                assert l >= line - tile["w"] and u <= line + tile["w"]
    result = lattice_slice(2, -25, -1)
    assert (2, 0) not in result["points"]
    assert {(-20, 0), (-5, 0)} <= set(result["points"])
    n = 10**13 + 1  # 2 mod3
    lo, hi = 6 * 10**12, 6 * 10**12 + 3000
    result = lattice_slice(n, lo, hi, tile_width=256)
    s, numerator = 6 * n + 3, 36 * n**3 + 54 * n**2 + 27 * n - 58
    found = {(x, t) for x, t in result["points"] if x * (t*t - (s+x)**2) == numerator}
    assert found == brute(n, lo, hi)
    assert result["stats"]["certified_tiles"] > 0
    # A verified large nonprimitive control. The unscaled triple sums to3;
    # scaling by5 gives375, and choosing its same-parity pair gives rational y.
    u, v, w = 569936821221962380720, -569936821113563493509, -472715493453327032
    assert u**3 + v**3 + w**3 == 3
    a_cube, b_cube, c_cube = 5*u, 5*w, 5*v
    total = a_cube + b_cube + c_cube
    control_n = (total - 3) // 6
    control_x = -(a_cube + b_cube) // 2
    control_t = abs((b_cube - a_cube) // 2)
    control = lattice_slice(control_n, control_x - 128, control_x + 128, tile_width=64)
    assert (control_x, control_t) in control["points"]
    assert (control_x, control_t) not in control["fallback_points"]
    try:
        lattice_slice(2, -100, 100, max_nodes=1)
    except WorkLimitError as exc:
        assert exc.stats["complete"] is False
    else:
        raise AssertionError("Expected an explicit work-limit error")
    print("Certified lattice checks passed.")
    print("Large-coordinate stats:", result["stats"])




